 * rule of 2026-11-14 let a closure write a captured cell as long as the cell stayed inside the
 * call that declared it. The author withdrew that premise on 2026-11-16 (design principles 3.5:
 * a closure captures a value, so it cannot assign the name it captured), and today the write is
 * refused where it is written -- `SemanticDiag::ClosureAssignsCapturedName`, 0x03000213 -- so the
 * "legal half" this file was made for was the half that stopped being legal. The write moved out
 * of the closure, to the line before it is made, and the value `f17` reads is the same one:
 * `v16` is 0 either way (`0 / -2` is 0), so the program's answer is unchanged and the reading
 * these entries guard -- `direct_calls` -- is the same reading.
 *
 * What this entry guards, and what it does not (measured 2026-11-15 on 6c64f2042):
 *
 *   - it guards that `std::cxx::inline` still inlines: `direct_calls` reads 2 with no pass and 0
 *     under the pass (`std::stats`), so a pass that stops inlining turns this entry red;
 *   - it does NOT guard the place decision that was added. With that decision switched off,
 *     this program runs at exit 0 with no cycle and no `PassInternalError`, exactly as with it
 *     on: the ring is unobservable in this shape today, not gone. The byte-for-byte drawing
 *     comparison is on the sibling shape, test/cases/opt/inline_cycle/seed23_fold_inline_within_call.cml.
 */
